"OpenAI hacked Australian Medicare govt site, probed data providers (https://www.bleepingcomputer.com/news/security/openai-hacked-australian-medicare-govt-site-probed-data-providers/)\nciteturn1167view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn1165view7\",\"id\":19}); Total lines: 138\nL0: [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input: Search Site]\nL1: \nL2: [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: # OpenAI hacked Australian Medicare govt site, probed data providers\nL5: \nL6: By\nL7: ###### cite0†Bill Toulas L8: \nL9:   * September 24, 2026\nL10:   * 05:38 AM\nL11:   * cite1†0 L12: \nL13: cite2†Image: OpenAI hacked Australian Medicare govt site, probed data providers†www.bleepstatic.com L14: \nL15: OpenAI agents targeted public data providers in multiple countries, probing some for vulnerabilities and exploiting a security weakness in an Australian government portal while performing information-retrieval tasks as part of a research project.\nL16: Earlier today, Australian Prime Minister Anthony Albanese confirmed that the agents breached a Medicare statistics reporting portal operated by Services Australia, the government agency responsible for delivering health and social payments.\nL17: \nL18: The unauthorized access occurred on June 18 and allowed OpenAI agents to access public and non-public data.\nL19: Nonprofit research lab Transluce released a report on the activity based on analysis of public records from the URL scanning service urlquery.net. The findings showed that the AI agents used the service's remote browser system to retrieve data when direct access failed.\nL20: \nL21: The lab describes three cases that occurred between May and June that impacted the Australian Institute of Health and Welfare, Data USA , and the digital library of the University of New Mexico.\nL22: According to the report, the AI agents performed seven probes against the educational organization, including attempts to exploit SQL injection, command injection, and path traversal flaws, while trying to retrieve a photograph.\nL23: \nL24: In the case of Data USA, a platform for public U.S. government data, Transluce found evidence that the AI agents probed the service for multiple vulnerabilities after receiving errors from malformed queries related to the University of Iowa.\nL25: When targeting the Australian Institute of Health and Welfare, the AI agents checked for exploitable vulnerabilities, including a reflected cross-site scripting (XSS), after getting errors.\nL26: \nL27: The researchers say Cloudflare blocked the requests, but the agents still retrieved a public file from a pre-production server.\nL28: \nL29: cite3†Image: Activity timeline†www.bleepstatic.com Activity timeline\nL30: Source: Translucent\nL31: cite4†Transluce underlines†transluce.org that it found no evidence that any of the observed attempts succeeded, but cautioned that the public dataset is incomplete and that it cannot rule out that the agents used other, more private avenues.\nL32: \nL33: In a statement for BleepingComputer, an OpenAI spokesperson said that \"initial review suggests that much of the activity described in Transluce’s report overlaps with cases at varying stages of investigation in our ongoing review of misaligned model activity.\"\nL34: \"We’ve reached out to the University of New Mexico and Data USA and have been in communication with the Australian government about affected government websites,\" OpenAI says.\nL35: \nL36: However, the AI company added that it is prioritizing the most serious incidents before reviewing lower-severity activity, such as agents spamming websites. It expects the review to take months due to the scale of the effort and the need to review each case individually.\nL37: ### Australian govt. confirms breach\nL38: \nL39: In a press conference earlier today, Australian Prime Minister Anthony Albanese said that an OpenAI agent breached a Services Australia Medicare statistics portal, accessed public and non-public files, and wrote data to an internal server.\nL40: \nL41: Albanese explained that the incident occurred during research conducted by OpenAI on public medicine spending, and noted that protection layers were in place to stop the data requests, but the agent bypassed them.\nL42: “There were blocks clearly which were coming back telling the AI agent, no. The AI agent found a way around those blocks.” cite5†Albanese stated†www.pm.gov.au .\nL43: \nL44: “The model attempted alternative ways to obtain the info that it wanted, and this led to unauthorized access into some other areas.”\nL45: \nL46: cite6†iframe†www.youtube.com L47: The Prime Minister said that an investigation has been launched to determine if any other government systems were affected, but based on the evidence so far, the incident has not impacted any individuals.\nL48: \nL49: OpenAI found that during an internal evaluation, its models tried to look up answers and statistics on several Australian government websites and services, and took action the company did not intend.\nL50: Albanese also said that OpenAI did not inform Australian authorities about the unauthorized activity until September 10.\nL51: \nL52: An OpenAI representative told BleepingComputer that the company discovered the intrusion in August while investigating \"misaligned model activity.\"\nL53: \nL54: The company notified Services Australia on September 10, after validating the activity and investigating what information the agents accessed.\nL55: \"Our review found no evidence of patient records being accessed. The information accessed included aggregate health statistics and internal file names,\" a company representative told BleepingComputer.\nL56: \nL57: As the review of the agent's activity is ongoing, OpenAI is notifying impacted organizations and providing technical information to support investigations and help fix potential security vulnerabilities.\nL58: \nL59: Update [09:50 EST]: Added information from OpenAI statement received after publication.\nL60: ## cite7†Build your security blueprint for AI-powered attacks†hubs.li L61: \nL62: Join Mikko Hyppönen and security leaders from the NFL, CHANEL, and Atlassian for a two-hour digital summit on what AI-speed attacks change, what defenders should stop doing, and how to validate, decide, fix, and re-validate at machine speed.\nL63: \nL64: cite7†Save your seat†hubs.li L65: \nL66:   * cite8†Agentic AI L67:   * cite9†AI Agent L68:   * cite10†Artificial Intelligence L69:   * cite11†Australia L70:   * cite12†Data Breach L71:   * cite13†OpenAI L72:   * cite14†Security Breach L73:   *   *   *   *   * ##### cite0†Bill Toulas bill.toulas@bleepingcomputer.com\nL74: \nL75: Bill Toulas is a tech writer and infosec news reporter with over a decade of experience working on various online publications, covering open-source, Linux, malware, data breach incidents, and hacks.\nL76: \nL77:   * cite15†Previous Article L78:   * cite16†Next Article L79: ##### Post a Comment cite17†Community Rules L80: ###### You need to login in order to post a comment\nL81: \nL82: [Input]\nL83: \nL84: Not a member yet? cite18†Register Now L85: \nL86:   Upcoming Webinar\nL87: \nL88: Popular Stories\nL89: \nL90:   * cite19†ShinyHunters claims FBI hack, data theft in PeopleSoft zero-day breach L91:   * cite20†Microsoft reminds admins to migrate Entra ID users to passkeys L92:   * cite21†New Windows Defender zero-day blocks Microsoft antivirus updates L93: \nL94: S ponsor Posts\nL95:   * cite22†Build cyber resilience with Wazuh: The open-source SIEM & XDR for proactive protection†wazuh.com L96:   * cite23†AI is a data-breach time bomb: Read the new report†info.varonis.com L97:   * cite24†Overdue a password health-check? Audit your Active Directory for free†specopssoft.com L98:   * cite25†Daily detection, monthly validation, 12-hour clocks. Are you VDR & VER ready?†www.anecdotes.ai L99:   * cite26†Automate Onboarding and Access Reviews with No-Code IGA: See how it works†www.tenfold-security.com L100: ##### Follow us:\nL101: \nL102: ##### Main Sections\nL103: \nL104:   * cite27†News L105:   * cite28†Webinars L106:   * cite29†VPN Buyer Guides L107:   * cite30†SysAdmin Software Guides L108:   * cite31†Downloads L109:   * cite32†Virus Removal Guides L110:   * cite33†Tutorials L111:   * cite34†Startup Database L112:   * cite35†Uninstall Database L113:   * cite36†Glossary L114: \nL115: ##### Community\nL116: \nL117:   * cite37†Forums L118:   * cite38†Forum Rules L119:   * cite39†Chat L120: \nL121: ##### Useful Resources\nL122: \nL123:   * cite40†Welcome Guide L124:   * cite41†Sitemap L125: ##### Company\nL126: \nL127:   * cite42†About BleepingComputer L128:   * cite43†Contact Us L129:   * cite44†Send us a Tip! L130:   * cite45†Advertising L131:   * cite46†Write for BleepingComputer L132:   * cite47†Social & Feeds L133:   * cite48†Changelog L134: \nL135: cite49†Terms of Use - cite50†Privacy Policy - cite51†Ethics Statement - cite52†Affiliate Disclosure L136: \nL137: Copyright @ 2003 - 2026 cite27†Bleeping Computer^{®} LLC - All Rights Reserved\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNew Carbonato malware uses AI agents to hijack exposed Docker hosts (https://www.bleepingcomputer.com/news/security/new-carbonato-malware-uses-ai-agents-to-hijack-exposed-docker-hosts/)\nciteturn1167view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn1165view7\",\"id\":3}); Total lines: 123\nL0: [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input: Search Site]\nL1: \nL2: [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: # New Carbonato malware uses AI agents to hijack exposed Docker hosts\nL5: \nL6: By\nL7: ###### cite0†Bill Toulas L8: \nL9:   * September 24, 2026\nL10:   * 04:10 PM\nL11:   * cite1†0 L12: \nL13: cite2†Image: New Carbonato malware uses AI agents to hijack exposed Docker hosts†www.bleepstatic.com L14: \nL15: A new botnet malware called Carbonato is targeting insecure hosts running Docker daemons to install the Hermes Agent AI framework and take control.\nL16: \nL17: The malware features worm-like capabilities and was discovered in an unauthenticated Docker registry that contained nearly 60 repositories and 4.3 GB of image data.\nL18: ThreatDown researchers at cybersecurity company Malwarebytes retrieved operational evidence spanning October 2024 to August 2026. The archive also included details about the botnet and a separate campaign that distributed counterfeit cryptocurrency wallet apps.\nL19: \nL20: According to Malwarebytes, Carbonato spreads across Docker hosts with an API exposed on port 2375 without authentication.\nL21: \nL22: The malware connects to that API and instructs the daemon to launch a privileged container, giving it access to the host.\nL23: It then opens a reverse SSH tunnel, installs an SSH server with the operators’ key, and reports the new deployment through Telegram. At the same time, scripts set up cron jobs, systemd timers, rc.local, and OpenRC hooks for persistence.\nL24: \nL25: One notable aspect of the attack is that the AI agent framework Hermes Agent is installed on the hosts, using an agent named “GH0ST,” with instructions that overwrite the default ‘SOUL.md’ persona file.\nL26: cite3†Image: The GH0ST agent instructions†www.bleepstatic.com The GH0ST agent instructions\nL27: Source: ThreatDown\nL28: \nL29: Hermes has been cite4†extensively abused in malicious cite5†cyber-operations recently . Recently, cybersecurity company Gambit documented a large-scale card-skimming operation that cite6†stole 600.000 credit card details .\nL30: In the case of Carbonato, Hermes handles task commands received through Telegram, including collecting AI API keys, SSH credentials, access tokens, and other data, running commands, and sending back the results.\nL31: \nL32: The researchers describe this as an operator-driven process involving an “interactive command loop” exchange.\nL33: “The​ ​model​ ​interprets​ ​the​ ​task,​ ​writes​ ​terminal​ ​commands,​ ​reads​ ​the​ ​output,​ ​and​ ​decides​​ what ​​to​​ do ​​next,” cite7†ThreatDown researchers note†www.threatdown.com .\nL34: \nL35: “​The​​ agent ​​runs ​​those​​ commands ​​on ​​the ​​victim​​ and​ ​returns​ ​its​ ​report​ ​to​ ​the​ ​Telegram​ ​chat​ ​that​ ​also​ ​receives​ ​deployment​ ​reports.​​”\nL36: The malware’s worm-like capability allow it to spread to other exposed Docker daemons and is handled by scripts that scan networks attached to the host every five minutes.\nL37: \nL38: Each new compromise pulls the implant from the registry, launches the same privileged container, and enters the persistence and scanning loop.\nL39: \nL40: ThreatDown could not attribute Carbonato to any known threat clusters, but based on various evidence, points to Costa Rica as a possible location of the operator.\nL41: To prevent infection, the researchers recommend keeping Docker daemon APIs off the network and requiring authentication on registries.\nL42: \nL43: Signs of Carbonato attacks include a GH0ST persona file, the CARBONATO_API_KEY setting, unexpected Telegram traffic, and reverse SSH tunnels toward AS262145.\nL44: ## cite8†Build your security blueprint for AI-powered attacks†hubs.li L45: \nL46: Join Mikko Hyppönen and security leaders from the NFL, CHANEL, and Atlassian for a two-hour digital summit on what AI-speed attacks change, what defenders should stop doing, and how to validate, decide, fix, and re-validate at machine speed.\nL47: \nL48: cite8†Save your seat†hubs.li L49: \nL50:   * cite9†AI L51:   * cite10†AI Agent L52:   * cite11†Artificial Intelligence L53:   * cite12†Botnet L54:   * cite13†Carbonato L55:   * cite14†Docker L56:   * cite15†Hermes L57:   * cite16†Malware L58:   *   *   *   *   * ##### cite0†Bill Toulas bill.toulas@bleepingcomputer.com\nL59: \nL60: Bill Toulas is a tech writer and infosec news reporter with over a decade of experience working on various online publications, covering open-source, Linux, malware, data breach incidents, and hacks.\nL61: \nL62:   * cite17†Previous Article L63:   * cite18†Next Article L64: ##### Post a Comment cite19†Community Rules L65: ###### You need to login in order to post a comment\nL66: \nL67: [Input]\nL68: \nL69: Not a member yet? cite20†Register Now L70: \nL71:   Upcoming Webinar\nL72: \nL73: Popular Stories\nL74: \nL75:   * cite21†ShinyHunters claims FBI hack, data theft in PeopleSoft zero-day breach L76:   * cite22†Microsoft reminds admins to migrate Entra ID users to passkeys L77:   * cite23†New Windows Defender zero-day blocks Microsoft antivirus updates L78: \nL79: S ponsor Posts\nL80:   * cite24†Daily detection, monthly validation, 12-hour clocks. Are you VDR & VER ready?†www.anecdotes.ai L81:   * cite25†AI is a data-breach time bomb: Read the new report†info.varonis.com L82:   * cite26†Overdue a password health-check? Audit your Active Directory for free†specopssoft.com L83:   * cite27†Automate Onboarding and Access Reviews with No-Code IGA: See how it works†www.tenfold-security.com L84:   * cite28†Build cyber resilience with Wazuh: The open-source SIEM & XDR for proactive protection†wazuh.com L85: ##### Follow us:\nL86: \nL87: ##### Main Sections\nL88: \nL89:   * cite29†News L90:   * cite30†Webinars L91:   * cite31†VPN Buyer Guides L92:   * cite32†SysAdmin Software Guides L93:   * cite33†Downloads L94:   * cite34†Virus Removal Guides L95:   * cite35†Tutorials L96:   * cite36†Startup Database L97:   * cite37†Uninstall Database L98:   * cite38†Glossary L99: \nL100: ##### Community\nL101: \nL102:   * cite39†Forums L103:   * cite40†Forum Rules L104:   * cite41†Chat L105: \nL106: ##### Useful Resources\nL107: \nL108:   * cite42†Welcome Guide L109:   * cite43†Sitemap L110: ##### Company\nL111: \nL112:   * cite44†About BleepingComputer L113:   * cite45†Contact Us L114:   * cite46†Send us a Tip! L115:   * cite47†Advertising L116:   * cite48†Write for BleepingComputer L117:   * cite49†Social & Feeds L118:   * cite50†Changelog L119: \nL120: cite51†Terms of Use - cite52†Privacy Policy - cite53†Ethics Statement - cite54†Affiliate Disclosure L121: \nL122: Copyright @ 2003 - 2026 cite29†Bleeping Computer^{®} LLC - All Rights Reserved\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nText-to-speech generation (TTS)  |  Gemini API  |  Google AI for Developers (https://ai.google.dev/gemini-api/docs/speech-generation)\nciteturn1167view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn1165view2\",\"id\":12}); Total lines: 1240\nL0: [Button: ]\nL1: \nL2: [Input: Search]\nL3: \nL4: `/`\nL5: \nL6:   * English\nL7:   * Deutsch\nL8:   * Español – América Latina\nL9:   * Français\nL10:   * Indonesia\nL11:   * Italiano\nL12:   * Polski\nL13:   * Português – Brasil\nL14:   * Shqip\nL15:   * Tiếng Việt\nL16:   * Türkçe\nL17:   * Русский\nL18:   * עברית\nL19:   * العربيّة\nL20:   * فارسی\nL21:   * हिंदी\nL22:   * বাংলা\nL23:   * ภาษาไทย\nL24:   * 中文 – 简体\nL25:   * 中文 – 繁體\nL26:   * 日本語\nL27:   * 한국어\nL28: cite0†Get API key†aistudio.google.com cite1†Cookbook†github.com cite2†Community†discuss.ai.google.dev Sign in\nL29: \nL30: cite3†Docs cite4†API reference L31: \nL32: [Input: Filter]\nL33: \nL34: [Button: ]\nL35: \nL36:   * Get started\nL37: \nL38:   * Models\nL39:   * Lyria 3.5 & Lyria Clip\nL40: \nL41:   * Text-to-speech\nL42: \nL43:   * Robotics\nL44: \nL45:   * Core capabilities\nL46: \nL47:   * Image\nL48: \nL49:   * Video\nL50: \nL51:   * Speech and audio\nL52: \nL53:   * Thinking\nL54: \nL55:   * Agents\nL56: \nL57:   * Tools\nL58: \nL59:   * Live API\nL60: \nL61:   * Get started\nL62: \nL63:   * cite5†Thinking L64:   * Optimization\nL65: \nL66:   * Guides\nL67: \nL68:   * File input\nL69: \nL70:   * Logs and datasets\nL71: \nL72:   * Safety\nL73: \nL74:   * Frameworks\nL75: \nL76:   * Resources\nL77: \nL78:   * Migration\nL79: \nL80:   * cite6†Status†aistudio.google.com L81:   * Google AI Studio\nL82: \nL83:   * Google Cloud Platform\nL84: \nL85:   * Policies\nL86: \nL87: [Button: Send feedback ]\nL88: # Text-to-speech generation (TTS)\nL89: \nL90: The Gemini API can transform text input into single-speaker or multi-speaker audio using Gemini text-to-speech (TTS) generation capabilities. Text-to-speech generation is cite7†controllable , meaning you can combine structured turn metadata (`speech_metadata`) and inline vocal tags to guide the style, accent, pace, and tone of the audio.\nL91: The TTS capability differs from speech generation provided through the cite8†Live API , which is designed for interactive, unstructured audio, and multimodal inputs and outputs. While the Live API excels in dynamic conversational contexts, TTS through the Gemini API is tailored for scenarios that require exact text recitation with fine-grained control over style and sound, such as podcast or audiobook generation.\nL92: This guide shows you how to generate single-speaker and multi-speaker audio from text using cite9†Gemini 3.8 Flash TTS (`gemini-3.8-flash-tts`) and cite10†Gemini 3.8 Flash-Lite TTS (`gemini-3.8-flash-lite-tts`).\nL93: ## Before you begin\nL94: \nL95: Ensure you use a Gemini TTS model listed in the cite11†Supported models section. For optimal results, review cite12†When to use which model to select the best model for your workload.\nL96: \nL97: You may find it useful to cite13†test the Gemini TTS models in AI Studio†aistudio.google.com before you start building.\nL98: \nL99: Note: TTS models accept text-only inputs and produce audio-only outputs. For a complete list of restrictions specific to TTS models, review the cite14†Limitations section.\nL100: ## Single-speaker TTS\nL101: \nL102: To convert text to single-speaker audio with Gemini 3.8 TTS models, pass the verbatim transcript in `input`, attach turn-level styling using a `speech_metadata` annotation, and configure your voice in `generation_config.speech_config`. You can choose a voice from the prebuilt cite15†Voice options , the Extended Voice Library (`GET /v1beta/voices`), a custom cite16†Voice design ID (`voice_...`), or a cite17†Voice replication ID (`voice_...`, or optional stateless `voicekey_...`).\nL103: This example saves the default WAV output audio (`audio/wav`) from the model directly to a file:\nL104: ### Python\nL105: \nL106:     `import base64\nL107:     from google import genai\nL108: \nL109:     client = genai.Client()\nL110: \nL111:     interaction = client.interactions.create(\nL112:         model=\"gemini-3.8-flash-tts\",\nL113:         input=[{\nL114:             \"type\": \"user_input\",\nL115:             \"content\": [{\nL116:                 \"type\": \"text\",\nL117:                 \"text\": \"Have a wonderful day!\",\nL118:                 \"annotations\": [{\nL119:                     \"type\": \"speech_metadata\",\nL120:                     \"style\": \"cheerful and friendly\",\nL121:                 }],\nL122:             }],\nL123:         }],\nL124:         response_format={\"type\": \"audio\"},\nL125:         generation_config={\nL126:             \"speech_config\": [\nL127:                 {\"voice\": \"Kore\"},\nL128:             ]\nL129:         },\nL130:     )\nL131: \nL132:     with open(\"out.wav\", \"wb\") as f:\nL133:         f.write(base64.b64decode(interaction.output_audio.data))\nL134:     `\nL135: ### JavaScript\nL136: \nL137:     `import * as fs from 'node:fs';\nL138:     import {GoogleGenAI} from '@google/genai';\nL139: \nL140:     async function main() {\nL141:        const client = new GoogleGenAI({});\nL142: \nL143:        const interaction = await client.interactions.create({\nL144:           model: 'gemini-3.8-flash-tts',\nL145:           input: [{\nL146:              type: 'user_input',\nL147:              content: [{\nL148:                 type: 'text',\nL149:                 text: 'Have a wonderful day!',\nL150:                 annotations: [{\nL151:                    type: 'speech_metadata',\nL152:                    style: 'cheerful and friendly',\nL153:                 }],\nL154:              }],\nL155:           }],\nL156:           response_format: { type: 'audio' },\nL157:           generation_config: {\nL158:              speech_config: [\nL159:                 { voice: 'Kore' },\nL160:              ],\nL161:           },\nL162:        });\nL163: \nL164:        const audioBuffer = Buffer.from(interaction.output_audio.data, 'base64');\nL165:        fs.writeFileSync('out.wav', audioBuffer);\nL166:     }\nL167:     await main();\nL168:     `\nL169: ### Go\nL170: \nL171:     `package main\nL172: \nL173:     import (\nL174:         \"context\"\nL175:         \"encoding/base64\"\nL176:         \"encoding/binary\"\nL177:         \"log\"\nL178:         \"os\"\nL179: \nL180:         \"google.golang.org/genai\"\nL181:         \"google.golang.org/genai/interactions/models/interactions\"\nL182:         \"google.golang.org/genai/interactions/models/operations\"\nL183:     )\nL184: \nL185:     func saveWaveFile(filename string, pcmData []byte) error {\nL186:         f, err := os.Create(filename)\nL187:         if err != nil {\nL188:             return err\nL189:         }\nL190:         defer f.Close()\nL191: \nL192:         sampleRate := uint32(24000)\nL193:         numChannels := uint16(1)\nL194:         bitsPerSample := uint16(16)\nL195:         byteRate := sampleRate * uint32(numChannels) * uint32(bitsPerSample/8)\nL196:         blockAlign := numChannels * (bitsPerSample / 8)\nL197:         dataSize := uint32(len(pcmData))\nL198: \nL199:         f.WriteString(\"RIFF\")\nL200:         binary.Write(f, binary.LittleEndian, uint32(36+dataSize))\nL201:         f.WriteString(\"WAVEfmt \")\nL202:         binary.Write(f, binary.LittleEndian, uint32(16))\nL203:         binary.Write(f, binary.LittleEndian, uint16(1))\nL204:         binary.Write(f, binary.LittleEndian, numChannels)\nL205:         binary.Write(f, binary.LittleEndian, sampleRate)\nL206:         binary.Write(f, binary.LittleEndian, byteRate)\nL207:         binary.Write(f, binary.LittleEndian, blockAlign)\nL208:         binary.Write(f, binary.LittleEndian, bitsPerSample)\nL209:         f.WriteString(\"data\")\nL210:         binary.Write(f, binary.LittleEndian, dataSize)\nL211:         _, err = f.Write(pcmData)\nL212:         return err\nL213:     }\nL214: \nL215:     func main() {\nL216:         ctx := context.Background()\nL217:         client, err := genai.NewClient(ctx, nil)\nL218:         if err != nil {\nL219:             log.Fatal(err)\nL220:         }\nL221: \nL222:         generationConfig := &interactions.GenerationConfig{\nL223:             SpeechConfig: genai.Ptr(interactions.NewSpeechConfigUnion([]interactions.SpeechConfig{\nL224:                 {Voice: genai.Ptr(\"Kore\")},\nL225:             })),\nL226:         }\nL227: \nL228:         res, err := client.Interactions.Create(ctx, operations.CreateInteractionRequest{\nL229:             Body: operations.NewCreateInteractionRequestBody(interactions.CreateModelInteraction{\nL230:                 Model: interactions.Model(\"gemini-3.1-flash-tts-preview\"),\nL231:                 Input: interactions.NewInteractionsInput(\"Say cheerfully: Have a wonderful day!\"),\nL232:                 ResponseFormat: genai.Ptr(interactions.NewCreateModelInteractionResponseFormat(\nL233:                     interactions.NewResponseFormat(interactions.AudioResponseFormat{}),\nL234:                 )),\nL235:                 GenerationConfig: generationConfig,\nL236:             }),\nL237:         })\nL238:         if err != nil {\nL239:             log.Fatal(err)\nL240:         }\nL241: \nL242:         if res.Interaction.OutputAudio != nil && res.Interaction.OutputAudio.Data != nil {\nL243:             pcmBytes, err := base64.StdEncoding.DecodeString(*res.Interaction.OutputAudio.Data)\nL244:             if err != nil {\nL245:                 log.Fatal(err)\nL246:             }\nL247:             if err := saveWaveFile(\"out.wav\", pcmBytes); err != nil {\nL248:                 log.Fatal(err)\nL249:             }\nL250:         }\nL251:     }\nL252:     `\nL253: ### REST\nL254: \nL255:     `curl -X POST \"https://generativelanguage.googleapis.com/v1beta/interactions\" \\\nL256:       -H \"x-goog-api-key: $GEMINI_API_KEY\" \\\nL257:       -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\nL258:       -d '{\nL259:         \"model\": \"gemini-3.8-flash-tts\",\nL260:         \"input\": [{\nL261:           \"type\": \"user_input\",\nL262:           \"content\": [{\nL263:             \"type\": \"text\",\nL264:             \"text\": \"Have a wonderful day!\",\nL265:             \"annotations\": [{\nL266:               \"type\": \"speech_metadata\",\nL267:               \"style\": \"cheerful and friendly\"\nL268:             }]\nL269:           }]\nL270:         }],\nL271:         \"response_format\": {\nL272:           \"type\": \"audio\"\nL273:         },\nL274:         \"generation_config\": {\nL275:           \"speech_config\": [\nL276:             { \"voice\": \"Kore\" }\nL277:           ]\nL278:         }\nL279:       }' | jq -r '[.steps[] | select(.type==\"model_output\") | .content[] | select(.type==\"audio\")] | last | .data' | base64 --decode > out.wav\nL280:     `\nL281: In the Python and JavaScript SDKs, you can retrieve generated audio data by using the `interaction.output_audio` convenience property, which returns the last generated audio block (in raw REST JSON responses, the base64-encoded audio is stored in `steps[].content[].data`). For details on convenience properties, see the cite18†Interactions overview .\nL282: ## Multi-speaker TTS\nL283: \nL284: For multi-speaker dialogue, configure two speakers in `speech_config.speakers` and pass each turn as a separate text item with a `speech_metadata` annotation specifying the `speaker` and optional turn-level `style`. Use `\"mode\": \"conversational\"` for natural turn-taking cadence:\nL285: ### Python\nL286: \nL287:     `import base64\nL288:     from google import genai\nL289: \nL290:     client = genai.Client()\nL291: \nL292:     interaction = client.interactions.create(\nL293:         model=\"gemini-3.8-flash-tts\",\nL294:         input=[{\nL295:             \"type\": \"user_input\",\nL296:             \"content\": [\nL297:                 {\nL298:                     \"type\": \"text\",\nL299:                     \"text\": \"How's it going today Jane?\",\nL300:                     \"annotations\": [{\nL301:                         \"type\": \"speech_metadata\",\nL302:                         \"speaker\": \"Joe\",\nL303:                         \"style\": \"cheerful and friendly\",\nL304:                     }],\nL305:                 },\nL306:                 {\nL307:                     \"type\": \"text\",\nL308:                     \"text\": \"Not too bad, how about you? Ready to test these new voices?\",\nL309:                     \"annotations\": [{\nL310:                         \"type\": \"speech_metadata\",\nL311:                         \"speaker\": \"Jane\",\nL312:                         \"style\": \"calm and relaxed\",\nL313:                     }],\nL314:                 },\nL315:             ],\nL316:         }],\nL317:         response_format={\"type\": \"audio\"},\nL318:         generation_config={\nL319:             \"speech_config\": {\nL320:                 \"mode\": \"conversational\",\nL321:                 \"speakers\": [\nL322:                     {\"speaker\": \"Joe\", \"voice\": \"Puck\"},\nL323:                     {\"speaker\": \"Jane\", \"voice\": \"Kore\"},\nL324:                 ],\nL325:             }\nL326:         },\nL327:     )\nL328: \nL329:     with open(\"out.wav\", \"wb\") as f:\nL330:         f.write(base64.b64decode(interaction.output_audio.data))\nL331:     `\nL332: ### JavaScript\nL333: \nL334:     `import * as fs from 'node:fs';\nL335:     import {GoogleGenAI} from '@google/genai';\nL336: \nL337:     async function main() {\nL338:        const client = new GoogleGenAI({});\nL339: \nL340:        const interaction = await client.interactions.create({\nL341:           model: 'gemini-3.8-flash-tts',\nL342:           input: [{\nL343:              type: 'user_input',\nL344:              content: [\nL345:                 {\nL346:                    type: 'text',\nL347:                    text: \"How's it going today Jane?\",\nL348:                    annotations: [{\nL349:                       type: 'speech_metadata',\nL350:                       speaker: 'Joe',\nL351:                       style: 'cheerful and friendly',\n"
